{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall not make for yourselves idols.</b> Corresponding to this one who is sold to a gentile — lest he say, 'Since my master violates sexual prohibitions, I too will be like him; since my master worships idolatry, I too will be like him; since my master profanes Shabbat, I too will be like him' — therefore these verses were stated. And these parashiyyot too were stated in sequence: first he was warned about the seventh year, and if he coveted money and was suspected regarding the seventh year, he ends up selling his movable property — therefore it adjoins to it ״וְכִי תִמְכְּרוּ מִמְכָּר״ — (what is written there? ״אוֹ קָנֹה מִיַּד עֲמִיתֶךָ״ — a thing acquired from hand to hand) — he did not repent, he ends up selling his ancestral holding; he did not repent, he ends up selling his house; he did not repent, he ends up lending at interest — all these latter ones are harsher than the former ones — he did not repent, he ends up selling himself; he did not repent — it is not enough for him to be sold to an Israelite, but even to a gentile." "<b>And a stone of pavement.</b> A term of covering, as in ״וְשַׂכֹּתִי כַפִּי״ (Exodus 33) — that they cover the ground with a pavement of stones." "<b>To prostrate oneself upon it.</b> Even to heaven, because prostration with hands and feet outstretched is what it is, and the Torah forbade doing so except in the Temple (Megillah 22)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:08:34.674705076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}